#' rOpenSci statistical standards: met
#'
#' Standards of the General, Regression and Bayesian categories that
#' sheepdog meets. Tags may later move next to the code they describe.
#'
#' @srrstatsVerbose TRUE
#'
#' @srrstats {G1.0} The DESCRIPTION field cites the primary references for the methods (Miller et al. 2011; Chambert et al. 2015; Wood and Kahl 2024) with DOIs, and `inst/CITATION` gives the package citation with its Zenodo concept DOI.
#' @srrstats {G1.4} All exported functions are documented with roxygen2 (`man/` is generated by `devtools::document()`).
#' @srrstats {G1.5} The performance claims in the README and in `vignettes/performance.Rmd` come from the scripts in `dev/benchmarks/`, with cached outputs in `inst/benchmarks/` and source data pinned by MD5 in `inst/benchmarks/curiango-source-manifest.json`. The scripts are in the repository, not in the built tarball.
#' @srrstats {G2.3a} Option arguments are restricted with `match.arg()` (18 calls, e.g. in `select_clips()`, `fit_calibration()`, `calibrate()`, `export_occupancy_data()` and `occasion_draws()`); other option strings are checked by hand.
#' @srrstats {G2.4} Explicit conversions are used throughout; see G2.4a to G2.4e.
#' @srrstats {G2.4a} `as.integer()` converts verdicts to `tp` in `read_validations()` and recording hours in `select_clips()`.
#' @srrstats {G2.4b} `as.numeric()` converts confidences and probabilities, e.g. in `occasion_draws()` and `post_validation()`.
#' @srrstats {G2.4c} `as.character()` converts site identifiers, e.g. in `export_occupancy_data()` and `select_clips()`; `paste()` only builds keys.
#' @srrstats {G2.4d} `read_validations()` converts `site` and `species` with `as.factor()`.
#' @srrstats {G2.4e} Factors are converted back with `as.character()`, e.g. in `calibration_ppc()`.
#' @srrstats {G2.10} Single columns are extracted with `[[`, `$` or dplyr verbs, and subsets use `drop = FALSE`; `[, "x"]` is used on matrices only.
#' @srrstats {G5.1} The example data are installed with the package (`system.file("extdata", "example", package = "sheepdog")`); the simulators and the v1.2.0 reference fixtures ship with the tests.
#' @srrstats {G5.4a} Trivial and hand-computed cases are tested: perfect and calibrated predictions in `score_probabilities()`, a four-draw `ppc_table()`, and `first_sustained()` on hand-made vectors.
#' @srrstats {RE1.0} Models are specified with a standard R formula (lme4 syntax for random effects) in `fit_calibration()`, `compare_calibration_models()` and `calibration_quality()`.
#' @srrstats {RE1.4} The assumptions of the calibration (linearity on the logit scale, representativeness of the reviewed clips, standardised covariates) and their consequences are documented in `vignettes/calibration.Rmd` and in `calibration_prior()`.
#' @srrstats {RE3.0} Singular and convergence problems raise warnings for lme4 and brms fits (`fit_calibration()`), lme4 messages are passed on, and failed cross-validation folds are counted (`n_failed`) and warned.
#' @srrstats {RE3.1} All warnings can be suppressed with `suppressWarnings()`; the messages stay in `attr(fit, "sheepdog_messages")`, `lme4::isSingular()` and the optimiser information stay in the lme4 object, and `calibration_diagnostics()` returns the brms diagnostics.
#' @srrstats {RE4.0} `fit_calibration()` returns objects of the standard classes `glm`, `glmerMod` or `brmsfit`, with sheepdog attributes.
#' @srrstats {RE4.2} Coefficients are available through `coef()` (glm, glmerMod) and `brms::fixef()` (brmsfit).
#' @srrstats {RE4.4} `formula()` works on all three returned classes.
#' @srrstats {RE4.5} `nobs()` works on all three returned classes.
#' @srrstats {RE4.6} `vcov()` works on all three returned classes.
#' @srrstats {RE4.7} Convergence statistics are available through `attr(fit, "sheepdog_messages")`, `lme4::isSingular()`, the lme4 optimiser information and `calibration_diagnostics()`.
#' @srrstats {RE4.8} The response `tp` is kept in the model frame (glm, glmerMod) or `fit$data` (brmsfit).
#' @srrstats {RE4.9} Fitted values are returned by `fitted()` and `predict(type = "response")`, and P(TP) for new detections by `score_detections()`.
#' @srrstats {RE4.11} Goodness of fit is reported by `compare_calibration_models()` (AUC, log-loss, Brier score, calibration intercept and slope), `calibration_quality()` and `summary()`.
#' @srrstats {RE4.13} The model frame keeps `logit_conf` and the grouping variables, and `attr(fit, "sheepdog_levels")` keeps the factor levels.
#' @srrstats {RE4.17} Returned objects print with the default methods of glm, glmerMod and brmsfit.
#' @srrstats {RE4.18} `summary()` methods of glm, glmerMod and brmsfit apply; `calibrate()` writes the summary to `calibration_model.txt`.
#' @srrstats {RE6.0} Default `plot()` methods of the returned classes apply, and `plot_calibration()`, `plot_calibration_draws()`, `plot_calibration_ppc()` and `plot_calibration_curves()` plot the calibration.
#' @srrstats {RE6.1} Calibration curves are plotted by `plot_calibration()`, documented as its own function; generic `plot()` dispatches to the glm, merMod and brmsfit methods.
#' @srrstats {BS1.1} The input data are described in `read_validations()` and in `vignettes/bayesian-calibration.Rmd`, which shows the data and formula used.
#' @srrstats {BS1.2} The principles of the prior are described in `calibration_prior()` and in the section "The prior" of `vignettes/bayesian-calibration.Rmd`.
#' @srrstats {BS1.2b} `vignettes/bayesian-calibration.Rmd` explains how to specify the prior, with code and a prior predictive check.
#' @srrstats {BS2.15} Failed cross-validation folds are caught, counted in `n_failed` and warned, and `calibrate()` continues when threshold suggestion or `calibration_quality()` fails.
#' @srrstats {BS4.2} Model fits can be checked with `calibration_ppc()` (posterior predictive), `prior_predictive_calibration()`, `calibration_sensitivity()` (prior sensitivity) and `calibration_quality()` (held-out data).
#' @srrstats {BS4.5} A warning with remedies (longer chains, higher `adapt_delta`) is raised when diagnostics fail; `calibrate()` writes `calibration_diagnostics.csv`.
#' @srrstats {BS5.1} Factor levels are kept in `attr(fit, "sheepdog_levels")`, and classes and dimensions of the input in `fit$data`.
#' @srrstats {BS5.2} The prior is returned in `fit$prior` and by `brms::prior_summary()`.
#' @srrstats {BS5.3} `calibration_diagnostics(fit)` returns convergence statistics from the fitted object alone; `summary(fit)` reports R-hat and ESS.
#' @srrstats {BS5.5} `calibration_diagnostics(fit)` returns `ok` and `n_divergent`, and a warning is raised at fitting time.
#' @srrstats {BS6.0} Returned brms objects print with `print.brmsfit()`.
#' @srrstats {BS6.1} `plot.brmsfit()` applies, and `plot_calibration_draws()` and `plot_calibration()` plot the calibration.
#' @srrstats {BS6.3} `plot_calibration_draws()` plots posterior and prior calibration curves, and `plot_calibration_ppc()` plots posterior predictive checks.
#' @srrstats {BS6.4} `summary.brmsfit()` applies.
#' @srrstats {BS6.5} `plot.brmsfit()` shows densities and traces together.
#' @srrstats {BS7.4} Tests check that `p_tp_lower` does not exceed `p_tp` and that probabilities lie in [0, 1], the scale of the input confidences (`tests/testthat/test-bayes.R`).
#' @noRd
NULL

#' rOpenSci statistical standards: to do
#'
#' Standards not yet met, each with a note on the gap.
#'
#' @srrstatsTODO {G1.1} *Statistical Software should document whether the algorithm(s) it implements are:* - *The first implementation of a novel algorithm*; or - *The first implementation within **R** of an algorithm which has previously been implemented in other languages or contexts*; or - *An improvement on other implementations of similar algorithms in **R***. Gap: state in the README how the package relates to existing implementations (designs with known inclusion probabilities, hierarchical calibration and occupancy export are new; the per-species logistic calibration follows Wood and Kahl 2024).
#' @srrstatsTODO {G1.2} *Statistical Software should include a* Life Cycle Statement *describing current and anticipated future states of development.* Gap: add a Life Cycle Statement (stability, deprecation policy for the legacy v1/v2 names, planned work) to CONTRIBUTING.md and the README.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G1.3} *All statistical terminology should be clarified and unambiguously defined.* Gap: terms are defined where they are used in the vignettes, but there is no central glossary (precision, recall ceiling, inclusion probability, occasion, calibration intercept and slope, effective n, P(TP)).
#' @srrstatsTODO {G1.4a} *All internal (non-exported) functions should also be documented in standard [`roxygen2`](https://roxygen2.r-lib.org/) format, along with a final `@noRd` tag to suppress automatic generation of `.Rd` files or [`@keywords internal`](https://roxygen2.r-lib.org/reference/tags-index-crossref.html?q=keywords%20internal#null) if documentation is still desired.* Gap: about 170 internal functions carry plain comments, many in Portuguese; they need English roxygen blocks marked noRd.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G1.6} *Software should include code necessary to compare performance claims with alternative implementations in other R packages.* Gap: no comparison with an alternative R implementation (e.g. a hand-coded per-species `stats::glm` calibration).
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.0} *Implement assertions on lengths of inputs, particularly through asserting that inputs expected to be single- or multi-valued are indeed so.* Gap: length assertions exist for some arguments (`k_folds`, `conf`, `window_step`, `n_versions`, `seed`, `score_breaks`) but not for scalar arguments of `select_clips()`, `suggest_thresholds()`, `threshold_bootstrap()` and `calibration_ppc()`. Planned: shared `check_*()` helpers.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.0a} Provide explicit secondary documentation of any expectations on lengths of inputs Gap: standardise the argument documentation on expected lengths.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.1} *Implement assertions on types of inputs (see the initial point on nomenclature above).* Gap: data inputs of `fit_calibration()`, `compare_calibration_models()`, `suggest_thresholds()` and `select_clips()` are not checked for class or required columns.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.1a} *Provide explicit secondary documentation of expectations on data types of all vector inputs.* Gap: standardise the argument documentation on expected types.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.2} *Appropriately prohibit or restrict submission of multivariate input to parameters expected to be univariate.* Gap: unchecked scalar arguments (`threshold`, `n_top`, `target_precision`, `min_confidence`, `p_min`) accept vectors silently.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.3} *For univariate character input:* See G2.3a (met) and G2.3b (gap).
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.3b} *Either: use `tolower()` or equivalent to ensure input of character parameters is not case dependent; or explicitly document that parameters are strictly case-sensitive.* Gap: option arguments are case-sensitive and this is not documented.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.5} *Where inputs are expected to be of `factor` type, secondary documentation should explicitly state whether these should be `ordered` or not, and those inputs should provide appropriate error or other routines to ensure inputs follow these expectations.* Gap: `fit_calibration()` relies on `levels(validations$species)` without checking that `site` and `species` are unordered factors.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.6} *Software which accepts one-dimensional input should ensure values are appropriately pre-processed regardless of class structures.* Gap: one-dimensional arguments are not stripped of extra classes before use.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.7} *Software should accept as input as many of the above standard tabular forms as possible, including extension to domain-specific forms.* Gap: `data.frame` and tibble inputs work through dplyr, but inputs are not coerced at entry and `data.table` is untested.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.8} *Software should provide appropriate conversion or dispatch routines as part of initial pre-processing to ensure that all other sub-functions of a package receive inputs of a single defined class or type.* Gap: no single entry routine coerces tabular inputs to one class. Planned: an internal `as_sheepdog_table()`.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.9} *Software should issue diagnostic messages for type conversion in which information is lost (such as conversion of variables from factor to character; standardisation of variable names; or removal of meta-data such as those associated with [`sf`-format](https://r-spatial.github.io/sf/) data) or added (such as insertion of variable or column names where none were provided).* Gap: rows dropped for missing confidence and values clamped by the bounded logit are not reported with a message.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.11} *Software should ensure that `data.frame`-like tabular objects which have columns which do not themselves have standard class attributes (typically, `vector`) are appropriately processed, and do not error without reason. This behaviour should be tested. Again, columns created by the [`units` package](https://github.com/r-quantities/units/) provide a good test case.* Gap: columns with non-standard classes (e.g. `units`) are neither handled nor tested.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.12} *Software should ensure that `data.frame`-like tabular objects which have list columns should ensure that those columns are appropriately pre-processed either through being removed, converted to equivalent vector columns where appropriate, or some other appropriate treatment such as an informative error. This behaviour should be tested.* Gap: list columns are not handled.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.13} *Statistical Software should implement appropriate checks for missing data as part of initial pre-processing prior to passing data to analytic algorithms.* Gap: missing-data checks exist in `calibration_folds()`, `calibration_quality()` and `read_pamdp()`, but not in `fit_calibration()`, `select_clips()` or `suggest_thresholds()`.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.14} *Where possible, all functions should provide options for users to specify how to handle missing (`NA`) data, with options minimally including:* Gap: there is no option for how missing data are handled; either add an `na_action` argument or document a fixed per-function policy.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.14a} *error on missing data* Gap: only `calibration_folds()` and `calibration_quality()` error on missing data.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.14b} *ignore missing data with default warnings or messages issued* Gap: rows with missing data are dropped, mostly without a message.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.15} *Functions should never assume non-missingness, and should never pass data with potential missing values to any base routines with default `na.rm = FALSE`-type parameters (such as [`mean()`](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/base/html/mean.html), [`sd()`](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/sd.html) or [`cor()`](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cor.html)).* Gap: audit summaries reachable with missing inputs, or guarantee removal at entry (G2.13).
#' @srrstatsTODO {G2.16} *All functions should also provide options to handle undefined values (e.g., `NaN`, `Inf` and `-Inf`), including potentially ignoring or removing such values.* Gap: `confidence` is not checked for NaN, Inf or values outside [0, 1] at entry; the bounded logit clamps Inf silently.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G3.0} *Statistical software should never compare floating point numbers for equality. All numeric equality comparisons should either ensure that they are made between integers, or use appropriate tolerances for approximate equality.* Gap: grid lookups use integer indices and thresholds use rounded scores, but four floating-point `==` comparisons remain (`reviewers.R`, `select_clips.R`, `recall_ceiling.R`).
#' @srrstatsTODO {G4.0} *Statistical Software which enables outputs to be written to local files should parse parameters specifying file names to ensure appropriate file suffixes are automatically generated where not provided.* Gap: user-supplied file names in `list_recordings()`, `threshold_record()`, `warning_signs()` and `run_app()` are written without checking the extension.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.2} *Appropriate error and warning behaviour of all functions should be explicitly demonstrated through tests. In particular,* Gap: 64% of the stop/warning/message conditions are matched by tests (84% of errors, 66% of warnings, 15% of messages; strict matching).
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.2a} *Every message produced within R code by `stop()`, `warning()`, `message()`, or equivalent should be unique* Gap: 14 condition messages occur in more than one place.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.2b} *Explicit tests should demonstrate conditions which trigger every one of those messages, and should compare the result with expected values.* Gap: see G5.2.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.3} *For functions which are expected to return objects containing no missing (`NA`) or undefined (`NaN`, `Inf`) values, the absence of any such values in return objects should be explicitly tested.* Gap: no-NA checks exist for several outputs but not for `threshold_bootstrap()` or for `fit_calibration()` predictions in general.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.6} **Parameter recovery tests** *to test that the implementation produce expected results given data with known properties. For instance, a linear regression algorithm should return expected coefficient values for a simulated data set generated from a linear model.* Gap: no test checks that `fit_calibration()` recovers a known intercept and slope from `simulate_validation()`.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.6a} *Parameter recovery tests should generally be expected to succeed within a defined tolerance rather than recovering exact values.* Gap: see G5.6.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.6b} *Parameter recovery tests should be run with multiple random seeds when either data simulation or the algorithm contains a random component. (When long-running, such tests may be part of an extended, rather than regular, test suite; see G5.10-4.12, below).* Gap: simulations use a single seed.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.7} **Algorithm performance tests** *to test that implementation performs as expected as properties of data change. For instance, a test may show that parameters approach correct estimates within tolerance as data size increases, or that convergence times decrease for higher convergence thresholds.* Gap: no test shows estimates improving as the number of reviewed clips grows.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.8} **Edge condition tests** *to test that these conditions produce expected behaviour such as clear warnings or errors when confronted with data with extreme properties including but not limited to:* Gap: `fit_calibration()` does not validate zero-row input, non-binary `tp`, missing `logit_conf` or single-class `tp`.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.8a} *Zero-length data* Gap: zero-row inputs are tested for several functions but not for `fit_calibration()`, `calibration_quality()` or `threshold_bootstrap()`.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.8b} *Data of unsupported types (e.g., character or complex numbers in for functions designed only for numeric data)* Gap: no test with a character `confidence` or non-binary `tp` passed to the calibration functions.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.8c} *Data with all-`NA` fields or columns or all identical fields or columns* Gap: all-TP or all-FP data and constant confidence are not tested in `fit_calibration()`.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.8d} *Data outside the scope of the algorithm (for example, data with more fields (columns) than observations (rows) for some regression algorithms)* Gap: singular fits are tested; separation and more random-effect levels than data are not.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.9} **Noise susceptibility tests** *Packages should test for expected stochastic behaviour, such as through the following conditions:* Gap: no noise-susceptibility tests.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.9a} *Adding trivial noise (for example, at the scale of `.Machine$double.eps`) to data does not meaningfully change results* Gap: no test perturbs inputs by machine epsilon.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.9b} *Running under different random seeds or initial conditions does not meaningfully change results* Gap: the same seed is tested to give the same result, but different seeds are not tested to give similar results.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.10} *Extended tests should included and run under a common framework with other tests but be switched on by flags such as as a `<MYPKG>_EXTENDED_TESTS="true"` environment variable.* - The extended tests can be then run automatically by GitHub Actions for example by adding the following to the `env` section of the workflow: Gap: two brms tests are gated by `SHEEPDOG_TEST_BRMS` and never run in CI. Planned: `SHEEPDOG_EXTENDED_TESTS` with the old name as alias, and an opt-in CI job.
#' @srrstatsTODO {G5.12} *Any conditions necessary to run extended tests such as platform requirements, memory, expected runtime, and artefacts produced that may need manual inspection, should be described in developer documentation such as a `CONTRIBUTING.md` or `tests/README.md` file.* Gap: the conditions for running extended tests (environment variable, brms with a Stan backend, run time) are not documented.
#' @srrstatsTODO {RE1.1} *Regression Software should document how formula interfaces are converted to matrix representations of input data.* Gap: document that formulas are converted to model matrices by `stats::glm()`, `lme4::glmer()` or `brms::brm()`.
#' @srrstatsTODO {RE1.2} *Regression Software should document expected format (types or classes) for inputting predictor variables, including descriptions of types or classes which are not accepted.* Gap: document the columns required by `fit_calibration()` and check them (formula class, columns present, `tp` in 0/1, factor `site` and `species`).
