#' NA_standards
#'
#' Standards that do not apply to sheepdog, with the reason.
#'
#' @srrstatsNA {G2.14c} Imputing reviewer verdicts or classifier confidences would bias the precision and calibration estimates that the package exists to provide, so missing values are never replaced.
#' @srrstatsNA {G3.1} The package computes no covariances; variance components come from model fits in lme4 or brms.
#' @srrstatsNA {G3.1a} The package computes no covariances (see G3.1).
#' @srrstatsNA {G5.0} No standard reference data sets exist for the validation and calibration of acoustic classifier outputs. Tests use the package example data (`inst/extdata/example`) and surveys simulated with known generating parameters (`tests/testthat/helper-simulate.R`).
#' @srrstatsNA {G5.4c} There are no published numeric outputs of these methods to store as test values.
#' @srrstatsNA {G5.11} Extended tests use only data shipped with the package; nothing is downloaded.
#' @srrstatsNA {G5.11a} No downloads are needed (see G5.11).
#' @srrstatsNA {RE2.2} Clips without a verdict are not training data: calibration is fitted on reviewed clips only and then used to predict P(TP) for every detection, which is the purpose of the package.
#' @srrstatsNA {RE4.1} Optional standard. A model object is always fitted, because convergence diagnostics run immediately after fitting.
#' @srrstatsNA {RE4.14} The package makes no forecasts or temporal extrapolation; it predicts P(TP) at observed scores.
#' @srrstatsNA {RE4.15} No forecasts (see RE4.14).
#' @srrstatsNA {RE6.3} No forecasts (see RE4.14).
#' @srrstatsNA {RE7.1a} With a binary response a noiseless relation is complete separation, for which logistic regression does not converge, so speed with noiseless data is not a meaningful comparison.
#' @srrstatsNA {RE7.4} No forecasts (see RE4.14).
#' @srrstatsNA {BS1.0} The package documentation does not use the term "hyperparameter" for the calibration priors; it appears only in `build_training_set()`, about the autotune of BirdNET-Analyzer training.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS1.5} A single composite convergence check is used (rank-normalised R-hat, bulk and tail ESS, divergences), so there are no alternative checkers to compare.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS2.3} Priors are `brmsprior` objects, one row per parameter class, not vectors of distributional parameters; brms validates them against the model parameters.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS2.4} brms matches each prior to the parameter classes of the model, and `calibration_prior()` only creates classes that exist in the model.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS2.5} Numerical validity of prior parameters is checked by brms and Stan when the model is built.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS2.9} Seeding is delegated to Stan through brms: one seed per fit, with distinct streams per chain.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS2.10} The package does not accept seeds per chain; a scalar seed is passed to brms.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS2.11} The package has no starting-value parameter of its own; `init` belongs to `brms::brm()`.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS3.2} With the single default predictor `logit_conf`, perfect collinearity arises only from user covariates, where the appropriate response is an error (BS3.1) rather than an alternative routine.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS4.1} The package implements no sampler of its own.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS4.4} Stan runs a fixed number of iterations; there is no stopping on convergence.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS4.6} No convergence checker with automatic stopping.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS4.7} No convergence checker with automatic stopping; the thresholds `rhat_max` and `ess_min` only classify a fit.
#' @srrstatsNA {BS5.4} A single composite convergence check is used (see BS1.5).
#' @noRd
